#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172690} :activity/id #uuid "8a73ceeb-8937-5609-a6e7-41612c249289" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050146 :topic/id #uuid "30c306d4-7422-557a-a542-cedff2b28c58" :topic/math-academy-id 3424} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172690 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173263 :tutorial/content "We recently defined two *finite* sets $A$ and $B$ as having the *same cardinality* if they contain the same number of elements. \n\nWhile this might seem a reasonable definition, we'd like to extend the concept of cardinality to infinite sets. Unfortunately, this definition will not do since counting the elements of an infinite set is impossible. \n\nTo get around this difficulty, we need a revised definition. Here it is:\n\n*Two set $A$ and $B$ have the **same cardinality** if there exists a bijection of $A$ onto $B.$ In other words, there is a one-to-one correspondence between the elements of the sets. Otherwise, if no such bijection exists, the sets are said to have **unequal cardinalities**.*\n\nAs before, we write $|A|= |B|$ when $A$ and $B$ have equal cardinalities, and $|A|\\neq |B|$ otherwise.\n\nFor finite sets, the situation is pretty simple. If two sets $A$ and $B$ have equal cardinality, then there exists a mapping that uniquely pairs each element of $A$ with an element of $B.$ This is equivalent to saying that $A$ and $B$ contain the same number of elements. \n\nFor example, the sets $A = \\{ a,b,c, d \\}$ and $B = \\{ 0, 1, 2, 3 \\}$ each have a cardinality of $4.$ The corresponding bijection might be the following:\n\n![](images/d8/d8d2c9de97d811be0e38ec1512546a399ebc3ee299934746a8d47bf30354b7b1.png)\n\n   \n\nIn contrast, for the sets $C = \\{ \\triangle \\}$ and $D = \\{ \\clubsuit, \\spadesuit \\},$ no bijections exist between these sets since they have different numbers of elements. Therefore, they have unequal cardinalities.\n\nFor infinite sets, it's a bit more complicated. However, we have the following fact to get us started:\n\n*If a set $A$ is finite and another set $B$ is infinite, then these sets have unequal cardinalities.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "aa23838f-1c70-54b6-a3f5-d7b92a50ad44" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8225 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "99a3bd0e-6210-52db-a6b7-e78f36de1139" :step/math-academy-id 146473 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172691}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172691 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171919 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171718 :question/id #uuid "23214c12-0d2f-54f1-8d27-d6a70406ff58" :question/math-academy-id "e-13173"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "308a8c68-628a-522f-9545-17a7165aefdf" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050254}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414416} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414426}] :knowledge-point/title "Finite Vs. Infinite Sets"} :step/id #uuid "e8a779b4-fed1-5e02-a9a4-d798f96442c2" :step/math-academy-id 146474 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172692}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171920 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171719 :question/id #uuid "1f394047-302d-561b-bae6-b88a881d68f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13180"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13fe2373-2094-5193-8e37-541d3a27e5e1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050157}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414427}] :knowledge-point/title "Using Arithmetic Sequences to Cons